rows=[
('Cyrus permits return','Cyrus announces temple rebuilding. Families prepare to return, and Sheshbazzar receives the temple vessels.','Restoration depends on people, material support, and imperial permission. Sheshbazzar is not automatically identified with Zerubbabel.','cyrus,jeremiah,nebuchadnezzar,sheshbazzar,mithredath-treasurer','jerusalem,babylon'),
('The returning community','A register names returning families and temple workers. Some priestly families cannot establish their descent.','The list records claims to belonging and service. Shared names do not identify every ancestor with a famous later person.','zerubbabel,jeshua-priest,nebuchadnezzar,returning-families','jerusalem,babylon'),
('Altar, foundations, joy, and grief','Jeshua and Zerubbabel lead renewed offerings and temple work. The foundation ceremony brings both celebration and grief.','Communal rebuilding can contain different memories and emotions. The Levite Jeshua in verse 9 remains distinct from the priest.','jeshua-priest,zerubbabel,moses,david,asaph-singer,cyrus','jerusalem,sidon,tyre,lebanon,joppa'),
('Opposition and royal letters','An offer of help is rejected. Opposition and letters to Persian rulers interrupt rebuilding before work resumes under Darius.','The chapter groups opposition across different reigns. Its letter sequence does not provide a simple continuous chronology.','zerubbabel,jeshua-priest,cyrus,darius-ezra,ahasuerus-ezra,artaxerxes-ezra,mithredath-writer,rehum-chancellor,shimshai','jerusalem,samaria'),
('Prophets and an official inquiry','Haggai and Zechariah encourage rebuilding. Tattenai requests a search for Cyrus’s decree, and the leaders explain their account.','Local testimony and imperial records shape the dispute. Sheshbazzar’s earlier foundation work is recalled without resolving every identity question.','haggai,zechariah-prophet,zerubbabel,jeshua-priest,tattenai,shethar-bozenai,sheshbazzar,cyrus,nebuchadnezzar,darius-ezra','jerusalem,babylon'),
('The decree, dedication, and Passover','The archive search supports rebuilding. Darius supplies protection and resources. The community dedicates the temple and celebrates Passover.','The account records welcome for people joining worship. Its royal punishment order is an ancient decree, not a present instruction.','darius-ezra,cyrus,artaxerxes-ezra,haggai,zechariah-prophet','jerusalem,babylon'),
('Ezra and the royal commission','Ezra is introduced through priestly descent and Torah study. Artaxerxes authorizes his journey, supplies, and administrative responsibilities.','Study, practice, and teaching are joined. Ezra departs from Babylon in the text; the guide does not substitute a different starting city.','ezra,aaron,moses,artaxerxes-ezra','babylon,jerusalem'),
('Preparing and accounting for the journey','Ezra gathers travelers and recruits temple workers through Iddo. Prayer precedes the journey, and the treasures are counted at arrival.','Responsibility includes preparation and accounting. Ahava and Casiphia have uncertain locations, so no invented route joins their markers.','ezra,artaxerxes-ezra,iddo-casiphia,sherebiah,hashabiah,meremoth,eleazar-phinehas,jozabad-ezra,noadiah-ezra,david','babylon,jerusalem'),
('Ezra’s prayer over community boundaries','Leaders report marriages across community boundaries. Ezra mourns and offers a prayer about guilt, mercy, and the surviving community.','The prayer expresses Ezra’s interpretation. Scholarly and Church readings differ about these boundaries and the spouses’ presumed religious practices.','ezra,foreign-wives-children','jerusalem'),
('The marriage investigation','Shecaniah proposes separation from foreign wives and children. Ezra summons an assembly, and an investigation names households involved.','The account gives little information about the affected women and children. Their individual beliefs, choices, and later lives remain unstated.','ezra,shecaniah-jehiel,jehohanan-eliashib,jeshua-priest,foreign-wives-children,marriage-objectors,shabbethai','jerusalem')]
