historical={1:'Eskenazi reads restoration within Persian rule and communal survival.',2:'Havea asks readers to consider returnees and the people who remained.',3:'Eskenazi treats temple rebuilding as part of reconstructing communal life.',4:'The letter sequence spans different reigns. The overview cautions against a simple continuous chronology.',5:'The narrative gives both the builders’ testimony and the officials’ inquiry.',6:'Imperial support and community worship are joined in the literary account.',7:'Eskenazi emphasizes scripture’s role in shaping a surviving community.',8:'The text names Babylon as the starting point. Ahava and Casiphia remain unlocated in this map.',9:'McClellan and Havea emphasize exclusion and communal boundaries. The Church lesson emphasizes idolatry and repentance.',10:'The interpretations differ. No source reviewed supplies the unnamed wives’ complete beliefs or later histories.'}
